[Music]  hello  and welcome to this discussion on the  land of the free my name is isabel  hilton and i'll be chairing the session  the land of the free is an idea that  along with the american dream is  drilled into the political consciousness  of every child and adult in the united  states  but after a recent rocky patch for the  american dream  president biden finally made it to the  inauguration to reassure us that  democracy  will prevail in some ways though  democracy  has never felt less secure millions of  trump voters  still refuse to accept the results of  november's  presidential election and there have  even been concerns from the left at  twitter's decision  to remove trump from its platform so are  these things indications  of the end of liberal democracy as the  dominant political ideal  are these troubles in the united states  signs that democracy itself is flawed  or will normal service indeed be  restored and the land of the free return  to find its claim of the global moral  high ground  strengthened through this recent turmoil  well to discuss this and other current  questions i'm joined by  jason brennan professor of strategy  economics ethics and public policy at  georgetown university  and the author of the ethics of voting  why not capitalism  and against democracy sarah elliott the  chair of republicans overseas uk  veteran of many republican campaigns who  has written about how she learned to  love donald trump  and by john sopo north america editor  for the bbc  and co-host of the bbc podcast americast  and author of the just published  unprecedented  welcome all now we're going to start our  uh  conversation and you have exactly three  minutes  uh to address the same question is  is democracy at this point a flawed  ideal is it something we should still be  holding up  jason perhaps we could start with you  yes well thank you and thank you for  having me this afternoon and my morning  uh i'm known as a critic of democracy  but i tend to think of myself as  democracy's best fan and the reason i'm  it's best fan is because i like it  exactly as much as it deserves  to be liked i tend to think of democracy  the way i think of the british heavy  metal brand uh  iron maiden i think they're wonderful  but they have a lot of albums that are  terrible in the early 90s  and similarly democracy might be the  best political system that we've come up  with so far  nevertheless having 70 years plus of  experience and  research studying what is going on in  democracy and how it actually functions  we've learned that it has built-in  systematic flaws and to illustrate that  let me start by talking about the theory  of democracy  which pretty much all of us learn in  primary school  in any democratic country that theory  says  what happens is voters start with a set  of interests and concerns and things  that they care about  they then go and learn how the world  works they discover the facts about  politics and what politics is able to do  they form a theory of some sort about  how the economy works and on the basis  of this they form  some sort of ideology and then on the  basis that ideology they look and find a  political party that best matches their  own ideals and values  and vote for that party or maybe vote  for a secondary party if that party is  too small or unlikely to win we all do  this at the same time  and then politicians who want power  supposedly will try to implement the  policies that  the modal voter the median voter or at  least the majority of some sort wants  and thus democracy is supposed to be  responsive to our interests  now if that's the story it's a wonderful  story but it's not really true  because what research has found over the  past 70 years is that the overwhelming  majority of voters are completely  uninformed they  know almost nothing about politics when  they consume news they look for  information that simply reaffirms  whatever it is they currently believe  and they're immune to new information  the overall majority of voters actually  are not even very ideological ideology  is actually the province of the elite  maybe only 10  of people in the uk or the us are  genuinely ideological and vote on that  basis  and the reason for this we join  political tribes in the same way we  largely joined sports  teams as fans why are we like this  because of bad incentives unfortunately  our votes don't count for much  if i vote one way or vote the other way  the same thing will happen everybody  knows this  and so we are what we call in economics  rationally ignorant and rationally  irrational  we know that our votes don't matter very  much unless we don't have an incentive  to use them  in a highly productive way it's a  built-in flaw inside of democracy  and we can't fix it unless we somehow  fix the incentive structure that makes  us ignorant  and irrational well incentivized to be  ignorant and irrational that's pretty  damning  uh sarah yes democracy  um well democracy is defined but by  government by the people especially the  rule of the majority  and on its face democracy doesn't seem  like a bad idea  it means that people get to choose their  government  and their elected officials and so forth  but where the idea of democracy may be  flawed  is it's tricky to implement it and  implement it well  so the founders of the united states  were very much aware of this  they wanted to create a system of how do  you respect  and give voice to the majority as well  as respect the minority  and have them included in the political  discussion  so that the minority will not be  or will not live under the tyranny of  the majority  and also there would be a truly  pluralistic society  and you're able to maximize individual  freedom  because with democracy the thought is  that freedom is then  the outcome or the consequence so how do  you control the passions  of the time so that decisions affecting  the whole  are well thought out that they're  reasoned and tempered with concern for  all  so that you prevent the whims and  the emotions and ignorance of the mob  from carrying the day  so the american founders looked at  history  and saw how well-intentioned pure  democracies had failed  in this regard and thus they created a  constitutional republic  so a republic is where government  supreme power resides and a body of  citizens entitled to vote  and is exercised by elected officials  and representatives  thus the u.s constitution created  institutions  to provide checks and balances on the  impassioned whims of the people  so we have three branches of government  you  have two senators from each state you  have representation by population  and it's why it takes a super majority  of the us  congress and three quarters of the  states to change the constitution  and it's why we have the electoral  college  this is all in an effort to secure  maximum freedom for the people  so that the will of the people is  genuinely protected  so the idea of a pure democracy is not  bad  but in order to have one where all  voices are heated or feel like they're  heard  um it's very difficult and that's why  the american founders created  the constitutional republic and as  benjamin franklin was famous for saying  when asked what he and the others had  just done  he said we've made a republic if you can  keep it  so is democracy flawed  yes this is why we've had to create  and the us constitution and bill of  rights and  and have a republic but i think the  essence of democracy is important  and is still the best form of government  we have  thank you sarah so we are i think fairly  clear that it's  complicated to build and maintain a  democracy but john i  bring back to the question of whether  the ideal itself  is flawed well  thank you very much and lovely to be  with you all um i'll start with the  churchill quote which i'm sure you're  all familiar  that is democracy is the worst form of  government except for all the others  and i think if you make that your  starting point  is it flawed yes is it a flawed  concept no because i still think that  going back to churchill  except when you look at all the other  options democracy is the best thing we  have got out there  and just let me talk a moment about some  of the things that are unique to a  democracy  or you know that are particularly  safeguarded in a democracy  and one that i think is particularly  true of the united states where you have  a first amendment which guarantees  uh freedom of speech uh you have the  rule of law you have protection of  minorities  you have safety nets and so all these  things are trying to create a civil  society  and where you are keeping as sarah has  just  spoken about the majority of the people  happily  happy and you don't have that tyranny of  the majority and so the american  constitution i think is the most  brilliantly designed document  of checks and balances where you can  keep all these things  sort of in balance and you are worried  the the founding fathers were  desperately worried about an over mighty  state  and that was what they felt that they  need to put in place  to have a sort of check on all of that i  want to focus  on one of the things because we're going  to have a conversation  which is very contemporary and one of  the things that i just  spoke about was the rule of law  and america's fragility  as a democracy came to the fore  in the wake of the november election  when donald trump maintained  that the election had been stolen  to continue watching this video click  the link in the top left or in the  description below  or visit iai dot tv for more debates and  talks from the world's leading thinkers  on today's biggest ideas

